Integration model and GPL boundary
----------------------------------
geneav does NOT link against libclamav and includes no ClamAV source code.
The geneav backend communicates with the ClamAV daemon (clamd) exclusively
over a network socket using ClamAV's native INSTREAM protocol
(see backend/src/main/java/com/geneav/scan/service/ClamAvScanEngine.java).

This arm's-length, inter-process socket boundary is the integration method
documented by the ClamAV project for use with commercial and closed-source
software:

  "Using clamdscan or communicating directly with clamd over a socket is fine
   for integrating ClamAV with commercial software, as this approach avoids
   direct linking to the GPL library."
  — https://docs.clamav.net/manual/Development/libclamav.html

Because geneav does not link libclamav and interacts with clamd only as a
separate process over the network, the GNU GPL v2 obligations attach to ClamAV
itself and do not extend to geneav's own application code.

If you redistribute a ClamAV binary or container image as part of a geneav
deployment, that ClamAV component remains under the GNU GPL v2: preserve its
copyright and license notices, keep it under the GPL, and make its source
available (the upstream source at https://github.com/Cisco-Talos/clamav
satisfies this for unmodified builds). Running ClamAV as a network service
that end users do not receive a copy of does not, by itself, trigger GPLv2's
distribution obligations.

ClamAV virus signature databases (CVD/CLD) are downloaded at runtime by
freshclam and are not redistributed as part of geneav.

